;; FH-TOPLAYER-003 — native focus, dismissal and NESTING, in a real browser.
;;
;; FH-TOPLAYER-001 pins what the tree says and FH-TOPLAYER-002 where each
;; property is legal. Neither can prove the thing the top layer exists for:
;; that the browser's own focus, inertness, light-dismiss and LIFO stacking
;; are what an author gets, and that the substrate contributes none of it.
;; Those are facts about a live `react-dom/client` mount read back off
;; `document`, and jsdom does not have them — there is no top layer to
;; promote anything into.
;;
;; Nesting is the row that earns its keep. React attaches refs bottom-up and
;; the browser decides what an opening popover closes from the DOM as it
;; stands at that instant, so a naive implementation opens the inner popover
;; first and then closes it by opening the outer one. Two auto popovers that
;; are NOT nested must collapse to one; two that ARE nested must both stay
;; open and must close together, outer-first. That pair of assertions is the
;; difference between using the platform's top layer and re-implementing it
;; badly.
;;
;; Dismissal is driven by `hidePopover()` rather than a synthetic key event:
;; an untrusted `KeyboardEvent` does not trigger native light dismiss, so a
;; synthetic Escape would prove nothing. The browser-initiated path being
;; proven is the one that matters — the node closes itself, the native event
;; reaches the author's handler, and the substrate writes no application
;; state on its behalf.
{:fh/id "FH-TOPLAYER-003"
 :fh/law
 "In a real browser the desired-state pair yields the platform's own behaviour: a modal dialog takes initial focus, makes the background inert and returns focus on close; two non-nested auto popovers are one-at-a-time while a nested pair stacks and closes together; and a browser-initiated dismissal reaches the author's handler without the substrate mutating any application state."

